Beryllium: at start the browser tries what the kernel does for a confined process, and says so

Purpose: plan/18 section 3.2, P4 (feature matrix S-KRN-03; plan/11 section
  4, step 3). The lockdown rests on the kernel in two ways a user cannot
  see from the browser: that capability mode exists, and that it holds.
  FreeBSD-SA-26:28.capsicum (9 June 2026) was a hole in the second:
  sigqueue() lacked the capability-mode restriction, so a confined process
  could signal an unrelated one. On a kernel without capability mode every
  renderer ends at its lockdown and the user sees a browser that shows
  nothing; on a kernel without that fix a locked-down renderer is confined
  less than Beryllium says. Fail closed is right for the first; silent is
  not right for either.
  The check is made by trying, not by comparing version numbers: the fix
  is in the supported branches at patch levels that a -CURRENT kernel, or
  one built locally, does not carry in its name
  (freebsd/ADVISORIES.md records the same about the development host).
  - base/posix/capsicum_rights.{h,cc}: CheckKernelForConfinement() forks a
    child that enters capability mode and calls kill(parent, 0) and
    sigqueue(parent, 0), both of which must fail there, and exits with the
    answer; signal 0 delivers nothing on any kernel. The child makes system
    calls only. The result is kept (KernelCheckResult()), and one line is
    logged for each fact that is not as the lockdown needs it.
  - sandbox/policy/freebsd/sandbox_freebsd.cc: the browser process makes
    the check once, in InitializeSandbox(), before it has other threads,
    and also when --no-sandbox is given (the kernel is the same).
  - chrome/browser/ui/webui/sandbox/sandbox_internals_ui.cc:
    beryllium://sandbox gains two lines, "kernel check: capability mode:"
    and "kernel check: a process in capability mode is refused signals to
    other processes (FreeBSD-SA-26:28.capsicum):".
  Not in this patch: a message in the browser's window. A user who starts
  the browser from a desktop menu does not see its log, so the plan asks
  for one; until then the page and the log say it.
Stage: 4 (Beryllium series). Depends on
  beryllium-kernel-attested-renderer.patch (base/posix/capsicum_rights),
  beryllium-capability-trap-report.patch (the place in
  sandbox_freebsd.cc) and beryllium-sandbox-status-page.patch.
Refreshed against: Chromium 156.0.8078.79, Helium 0.20.3 (2f81b9ca),
  FreeBSD ports 6ad3e0c8, using freebsd/tools/patch-workbench. Not yet
  compiled (written for build 0.20.3.22). base/posix/capsicum_rights.cc as
  patched type-checks whole against FreeBSD 16's headers with stand-ins for
  the Chromium headers it includes; the page's function was run on Linux
  with stand-ins and wrote the two lines.
Test: freebsd/tests/browser/tests/test_50_sandbox.py
  test_sandbox_page_matches_the_kernel requires both lines on a page from
  0.20.3.22 and, with the lockdown on, that they say "available" and "yes";
  freebsd/tests/capsicum-standalone/cap-oracle.c makes the same sigqueue
  probe outside the browser.
security-review: required (sandbox reporting; a fork in the browser process)

